174 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 639 0 R
/E (In an Arduino program, first we define the pins or variables. This means we give a name to a pin number so it is easy to use in the code. For example, if we write int ledPin = 5;, it means we are using pin number 5 and calling it ledPin.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [22]
>>
endobj
175 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 640 0 R
/E (Next comes the setup\(\) function. This part runs only one time when the Arduino is turned on. It is used to prepare the pins and start things like the Serial Monitor. Inside setup, we use pinMode\(\) to tell the Arduino what each pin will do. If we write pinMode\(ledPin, OUTPUT\);, it means the pin will send signals to control devices like an LED.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [23]
>>
endobj
176 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 641 0 R
/E (After that comes the loop\(\) function. This part runs again and again forever. Arduino keeps repeating whatever is written inside loop, so it can continuously control or check the circuit.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [24]
>>
endobj
177 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 642 0 R
/E (Inside the loop, we can use digitalWrite\(\) to turn an output ON or OFF. For example, digitalWrite\(ledPin, HIGH\); will turn the LED ON, and digitalWrite\(ledPin, LOW\); will turn it OFF.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [25]
>>
endobj
178 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 79 0 R
/E (So overall, the program first sets everything in setup\(\), then keeps running the loop continuously, controlling devices like LEDs using functions such as pinMode\(\) and digitalWrite\(\).)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [26]
>>
endobj
